# Express API

> Route and service patterns for {{project.name}} using Express.

## When to Use

Load this skill when creating or modifying API routes, services, or middleware.

## Route Pattern

```typescript
// {{structure.backend}}/routes/[resource].routes.ts
import { Router } from 'express';
import { asyncHandler } from '../middleware/async-handler';
import { validate } from '../middleware/validate';
import { [Resource]Schema } from '../validators/[resource].validator';
import * as [resource]Service from '../services/[resource].service';

const router = Router();

router.get('/', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const data = await [resource]Service.findAll(req.query);
  res.json({ data });
}));

router.get('/:id', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const data = await [resource]Service.findById(req.params.id);
  res.json({ data });
}));

router.post('/', validate([Resource]Schema), asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const data = await [resource]Service.create(req.body);
  res.status(201).json({ data });
}));

export default router;
```

## Service Pattern

- Services contain business logic, routes are thin controllers
- Services receive validated data and return domain objects
- Services throw typed AppError subclasses on failure

## Key Rules

- Every route must use `asyncHandler` wrapper
- Every POST/PUT/PATCH must validate request body
- Never access `prisma` directly in routes — always go through services
- Register routes in a central `routes/index.ts`

